{"module":"voting","voice":"female","duration":284.3,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"The fight for the vote","start":0.0,"end":1.32,"words":[[0.05,0.21],[0.21,0.53],[0.53,0.69],[0.69,0.86],[0.86,1.18]]},{"el":"intro","text":"The people who decide whether discriminatory 911 calls stay legal are elected.","start":2.12,"end":7.39,"words":[[2.17,2.3],[2.3,2.62],[2.62,2.75],[2.75,3.14],[3.14,3.39],[3.39,4.29],[4.29,5.52],[5.52,5.77],[5.77,6.03],[6.03,6.35],[6.35,6.42],[6.42,6.93]]},{"el":"intro","text":"That is why the vote has been fought over so fiercely for 150 years, and why it is the most powerful tool for passing the CAREN Act.","start":7.74,"end":16.12,"words":[[7.78,8.0],[8.0,8.15],[8.15,8.36],[8.36,8.51],[8.51,8.8],[8.8,9.01],[9.01,9.23],[9.23,9.45],[9.45,9.74],[9.74,9.95],[9.95,10.39],[10.39,10.53],[10.53,11.76],[11.76,12.12],[12.37,12.59],[12.59,12.81],[12.81,12.95],[12.95,13.1],[13.1,13.24],[13.24,13.6],[13.6,14.11],[14.11,14.32],[14.32,14.47],[14.47,14.83],[14.83,14.97],[14.97,15.34],[15.34,15.62]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Won: Reconstruction","start":16.47,"end":18.64,"words":[[16.52,16.9],[17.23,18.48]]},{"el":"s0","text":"The 15th Amendment (1870) barred denying the vote because of race.","start":19.34,"end":24.54,"words":[[19.41,19.56],[19.56,20.08],[20.08,20.75],[20.75,21.64],[21.9,22.13],[22.13,22.65],[22.65,22.8],[22.8,23.1],[23.1,23.47],[23.47,23.62],[23.62,23.99]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Within a few years, Black voters elected about 2,000 Black officeholders across the South, from sheriffs to state legislators.","start":24.89,"end":33.83,"words":[[24.94,25.32],[25.32,25.47],[25.47,25.69],[25.69,26.07],[26.34,26.64],[26.64,27.1],[27.1,27.63],[27.63,28.0],[28.0,28.69],[28.69,28.99],[28.99,29.82],[29.82,30.2],[30.2,30.35],[30.35,30.73],[31.0,31.3],[31.3,31.75],[31.75,31.91],[31.91,32.28],[32.28,33.27]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Hiram Revels of Mississippi became the first Black U.S. Senator in 1870.","start":34.18,"end":40.17,"words":[[34.18,34.65],[34.65,35.13],[35.13,35.29],[35.29,35.92],[35.92,36.4],[36.4,36.56],[36.56,36.87],[36.87,37.19],[37.19,37.59],[37.98,38.46],[38.46,38.62],[38.62,39.65]]},{"el":"s0","text":"(See the module on Reconstruction.)","start":40.52,"end":42.93,"words":[[40.56,40.71],[40.71,40.86],[40.86,41.32],[41.32,41.48],[41.48,42.55]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Taken: poll taxes, tests and terror","start":43.83,"end":47.51,"words":[[43.83,44.49],[44.82,45.2],[45.2,45.86],[46.19,46.66],[46.66,46.94],[46.94,47.31]]},{"el":"s1","text":"When Reconstruction ended, Southern states rewrote their constitutions to get around the 15th Amendment without naming race.","start":48.21,"end":55.3,"words":[[48.26,48.45],[48.45,49.26],[49.26,49.64],[49.86,50.17],[50.17,50.54],[50.54,50.92],[50.92,51.11],[51.11,51.92],[51.92,52.04],[52.04,52.23],[52.23,52.61],[52.61,52.73],[52.73,53.17],[53.17,53.73],[53.73,54.11],[54.11,54.48],[54.48,54.8]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Mississippi led in 1890 with poll taxes, literacy tests and an “understanding clause” that let white registrars pass whom they chose.","start":55.65,"end":65.83,"words":[[55.7,56.39],[56.39,56.65],[56.65,56.82],[56.82,57.78],[57.78,58.04],[58.04,58.39],[58.39,58.99],[59.3,59.99],[59.99,60.43],[60.43,60.69],[60.69,60.86],[60.86,61.81],[61.81,62.16],[62.16,62.42],[62.42,62.68],[62.68,63.03],[63.03,63.9],[63.9,64.16],[64.16,64.42],[64.42,64.68],[64.68,65.2]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Other tools were “grandfather clauses” (struck down in 1915) and all-white primaries (struck down in 1944).","start":66.18,"end":73.69,"words":[[66.23,66.45],[66.45,66.74],[66.74,66.89],[66.89,67.55],[67.55,67.99],[67.99,68.35],[68.35,68.65],[68.65,68.79],[68.79,69.75],[70.0,70.22],[70.22,70.66],[70.66,71.32],[71.32,71.69],[71.69,71.98],[71.98,72.13],[72.13,73.15]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Violence enforced the rest: in Ocoee, Florida, in 1920, Black residents who tried to vote were massacred.","start":74.04,"end":82.31,"words":[[74.09,74.69],[74.69,75.14],[75.14,75.29],[75.29,75.67],[75.93,76.08],[76.08,76.46],[76.72,77.32],[77.58,77.73],[77.73,78.79],[79.05,79.35],[79.35,80.03],[80.03,80.18],[80.18,80.55],[80.55,80.7],[80.7,81.0],[81.0,81.15],[81.15,81.75]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The results were stark.","start":82.66,"end":84.16,"words":[[82.74,82.86],[82.86,83.3],[83.3,83.42],[83.42,83.74]]},{"el":"s1","text":"In Louisiana, registered Black voters fell from about 130,000 in 1896 to about 1,300 by 1904.","start":84.51,"end":93.36,"words":[[84.57,84.7],[84.7,85.35],[85.57,86.15],[86.15,86.41],[86.41,86.8],[86.8,86.99],[86.99,87.25],[87.25,87.57],[87.57,89.05],[89.05,89.18],[89.18,90.15],[90.15,90.28],[90.28,90.6],[90.6,91.89],[91.89,92.08],[92.08,92.85]]},{"el":"s1","text":"At Alabama's 1901 constitutional convention, the convention president said its purpose was to “establish white supremacy in this State.”","start":93.71,"end":102.36,"words":[[93.77,93.91],[93.91,94.46],[94.46,95.29],[95.29,96.26],[96.26,96.95],[97.19,97.33],[97.33,97.95],[97.95,98.57],[98.57,98.78],[98.78,98.99],[98.99,99.33],[99.33,99.54],[99.54,99.68],[99.68,100.23],[100.23,100.51],[100.51,101.13],[101.13,101.27],[101.27,101.47],[101.47,101.89]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Won again: Selma and the Voting Rights Act","start":103.26,"end":106.17,"words":[[103.31,103.54],[103.54,103.92],[104.19,104.57],[104.57,104.8],[104.8,104.95],[104.95,105.41],[105.41,105.8],[105.8,106.02]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Organizers registered voters at the risk of their lives.","start":106.87,"end":110.18,"words":[[106.93,107.52],[107.52,108.11],[108.11,108.51],[108.51,108.64],[108.64,108.77],[108.77,109.03],[109.03,109.16],[109.16,109.36],[109.36,109.75]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Medgar Evers was murdered in 1963, and three Freedom Summer workers, James Chaney, Andrew Goodman and Michael Schwerner, in 1964.","start":110.53,"end":122.1,"words":[[110.58,111.01],[111.01,111.35],[111.35,111.6],[111.6,112.03],[112.03,112.2],[112.2,113.64],[113.94,114.19],[114.19,114.45],[114.45,114.96],[114.96,115.29],[115.29,115.8],[116.1,116.61],[116.61,117.2],[117.5,117.93],[117.93,118.44],[118.44,118.69],[118.69,119.2],[119.2,119.71],[120.01,120.18],[120.18,121.53]]},{"el":"s2","text":"On March 7, 1965 (“Bloody Sunday”), state troopers beat marchers, including John Lewis, on the Edmund Pettus Bridge in Selma.","start":122.45,"end":132.65,"words":[[122.5,122.66],[122.66,122.97],[122.97,123.45],[123.73,125.08],[125.08,125.47],[125.47,126.03],[126.31,126.7],[126.7,127.18],[127.18,127.42],[127.42,127.97],[128.25,128.88],[128.88,129.2],[129.2,129.6],[129.88,130.03],[130.03,130.19],[130.19,130.67],[130.67,131.07],[131.07,131.46],[131.46,131.62],[131.62,132.1]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Five months later Congress passed the Voting Rights Act.","start":133.0,"end":136.46,"words":[[133.03,133.32],[133.32,133.67],[133.67,134.03],[134.03,134.52],[134.52,134.8],[134.8,134.95],[134.95,135.37],[135.37,135.73],[135.73,136.01]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Its Section 5 required states and counties with a history of discrimination to get federal approval before changing voting rules (“preclearance”).","start":136.81,"end":146.14,"words":[[136.86,137.08],[137.08,137.52],[137.52,137.82],[137.82,138.4],[138.4,138.84],[138.84,139.06],[139.06,139.57],[139.57,139.79],[139.79,139.94],[139.94,140.45],[140.45,140.6],[140.6,141.63],[141.63,141.77],[141.77,141.99],[141.99,142.51],[142.51,143.02],[143.02,143.31],[143.31,143.97],[143.97,144.41],[144.41,144.71],[144.71,145.58]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The 24th Amendment (1964) and a 1966 Supreme Court ruling ended poll taxes.","start":146.49,"end":154.53,"words":[[146.53,146.7],[146.7,147.44],[147.44,148.18],[148.18,149.41],[149.69,149.94],[149.94,150.1],[150.1,151.5],[151.5,151.99],[151.99,152.23],[152.23,152.64],[152.64,153.05],[153.05,153.38],[153.38,153.96]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In Mississippi, Black voter registration rose from about 7% in 1964 to about 60% by 1967.","start":154.88,"end":164.77,"words":[[154.93,155.09],[155.09,155.79],[156.06,156.38],[156.38,156.77],[156.77,157.79],[157.79,158.1],[158.1,158.41],[158.41,158.8],[158.8,159.66],[159.66,159.82],[159.82,160.99],[160.99,161.15],[161.15,161.54],[161.54,162.48],[162.48,162.71],[162.71,164.2]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Weakened: Shelby County v. Holder (2013)","start":165.67,"end":169.4,"words":[[165.72,166.22],[166.47,166.82],[166.82,167.25],[167.25,167.46],[167.81,168.24],[168.24,169.02]]},{"el":"s3","text":"In 2013 the Supreme Court struck down the formula that decided which places needed preclearance, which ended preclearance in practice.","start":170.1,"end":179.48,"words":[[170.15,170.3],[170.3,171.15],[171.15,171.3],[171.3,171.76],[171.76,171.99],[171.99,172.38],[172.38,172.69],[172.69,172.84],[172.84,173.38],[173.38,173.61],[173.61,174.22],[174.22,174.53],[174.53,175.07],[175.07,175.45],[175.45,176.38],[176.65,176.95],[176.95,177.34],[177.34,178.18],[178.18,178.34],[178.34,178.95]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Texas announced its voter ID law the same day.","start":179.83,"end":182.99,"words":[[179.88,180.31],[180.31,180.81],[180.81,181.02],[181.02,181.38],[181.38,181.66],[181.66,181.8],[181.8,181.95],[181.95,182.23],[182.23,182.52]]},{"el":"s3","text":"In 2016 a federal appeals court struck down a 2013 North Carolina law, finding that it targeted Black voters “with almost surgical precision.”","start":183.34,"end":193.73,"words":[[183.4,183.55],[183.55,184.54],[184.54,184.7],[184.7,185.23],[185.23,185.61],[185.61,185.84],[185.84,186.22],[186.22,186.53],[186.53,186.68],[186.68,187.52],[187.52,187.75],[187.75,188.43],[188.43,188.66],[188.93,189.46],[189.46,189.69],[189.69,189.85],[189.85,190.38],[190.38,190.69],[190.69,191.14],[191.14,191.37],[191.37,191.91],[191.91,192.52],[192.52,193.2]]},{"el":"s3","text":"A 2019 civil rights report counted about 1,700 polling places closed in formerly covered areas between 2012 and 2018.","start":194.08,"end":204.18,"words":[[194.14,194.28],[194.28,195.2],[195.2,195.56],[195.56,195.91],[195.91,196.27],[196.27,196.76],[196.76,197.12],[197.12,198.68],[198.68,199.1],[199.1,199.6],[199.6,200.02],[200.02,200.17],[200.17,200.59],[200.59,200.95],[200.95,201.37],[201.37,201.8],[201.8,202.58],[202.58,202.79],[202.79,203.71]]},{"el":"s3","text":"In 2023, in Allen v. Milligan, the Court did uphold Section 2 of the act and required Alabama to draw a second district where Black voters could elect their choice.","start":204.53,"end":216.1,"words":[[204.58,204.73],[204.73,205.93],[206.19,206.35],[206.35,206.65],[206.65,206.87],[207.25,207.85],[208.11,208.26],[208.26,208.49],[208.49,208.71],[208.71,209.24],[209.24,209.69],[209.69,209.84],[209.84,209.99],[209.99,210.14],[210.14,210.36],[210.36,210.59],[210.59,211.19],[211.19,211.72],[211.72,211.87],[211.87,212.09],[212.09,212.24],[212.24,212.69],[212.69,213.29],[213.29,213.52],[213.52,213.82],[213.82,214.27],[214.27,214.5],[214.5,214.87],[214.87,215.1],[215.1,215.55]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Locked out: felony disenfranchisement","start":217.0,"end":219.76,"words":[[217.05,217.34],[217.34,217.63],[217.88,218.3],[218.3,219.59]]},{"el":"s4","text":"About 4 million Americans can't vote because of a felony conviction, and Black Americans are disenfranchised at more than three times the rate of others (Sentencing Project).","start":220.46,"end":230.65,"words":[[220.51,220.86],[220.86,221.01],[221.01,221.43],[221.43,222.08],[222.08,222.36],[222.36,222.65],[222.65,223.01],[223.01,223.15],[223.15,223.29],[223.29,223.72],[223.72,224.44],[224.69,224.9],[224.9,225.19],[225.19,225.83],[225.83,225.9],[225.9,226.97],[226.97,227.11],[227.11,227.26],[227.26,227.47],[227.47,227.69],[227.69,228.04],[228.04,228.19],[228.19,228.47],[228.47,228.61],[228.61,228.9],[228.9,229.54],[229.54,230.19]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Many of these laws were written after Reconstruction to target crimes officials thought Black people were likelier to commit.","start":231.0,"end":237.99,"words":[[231.06,231.36],[231.36,231.51],[231.51,231.73],[231.73,231.95],[231.95,232.1],[232.1,232.47],[232.47,232.77],[232.77,233.74],[233.74,233.89],[233.89,234.26],[234.26,234.71],[234.71,235.23],[235.23,235.45],[235.45,235.75],[235.75,236.12],[236.12,236.27],[236.27,236.87],[236.87,237.02],[237.02,237.46]]},{"el":"s4","text":"In 1985 the Supreme Court struck down part of Alabama's law for that reason (Hunter v. Underwood).","start":238.34,"end":245.28,"words":[[238.39,238.54],[238.54,239.7],[239.7,239.85],[239.85,240.31],[240.31,240.54],[240.54,240.93],[240.93,241.24],[241.24,241.47],[241.47,241.62],[241.62,242.24],[242.24,242.39],[242.39,242.55],[242.55,242.78],[242.78,243.16],[243.16,243.55],[243.55,243.78],[244.16,244.78]]},{"el":"s4","text":"In 2018 Florida voters restored voting rights to most people who had finished their sentences; the legislature then required them to pay all fines and fees first.","start":245.63,"end":255.74,"words":[[245.68,245.83],[245.83,246.69],[246.69,247.19],[247.19,247.62],[247.62,248.05],[248.05,248.49],[248.49,248.85],[248.85,248.99],[248.99,249.35],[249.35,249.71],[249.71,249.85],[249.85,250.07],[250.07,250.5],[250.5,250.71],[250.71,251.43],[251.68,251.83],[251.83,252.62],[252.62,252.84],[252.84,253.41],[253.41,253.63],[253.63,253.77],[253.77,253.99],[253.99,254.13],[254.13,254.49],[254.49,254.7],[254.7,254.92],[254.92,255.28]]},{"el":"h5","text":"Drawn out: redistricting","start":256.64,"end":258.32,"words":[[256.64,256.91],[256.91,257.18],[257.42,258.23]]},{"el":"s5","text":"District lines can “pack” voters of color into a few districts or “crack” them across many so their votes count for less.","start":259.02,"end":265.52,"words":[[259.08,259.62],[259.62,259.95],[259.95,260.16],[260.16,260.36],[260.36,260.76],[260.76,260.9],[260.9,261.17],[261.17,261.44],[261.44,261.57],[261.57,261.78],[261.78,262.38],[262.38,262.45],[262.45,262.72],[262.72,262.92],[262.92,263.26],[263.26,263.53],[263.53,263.73],[263.73,263.93],[263.93,264.27],[264.27,264.61],[264.61,264.74],[264.74,265.01]]},{"el":"s5","text":"The Supreme Court ruled in 2019 that federal courts won't police partisan gerrymanders, but racial gerrymanders can still be challenged under the Voting Rights Act.","start":265.87,"end":275.56,"words":[[265.92,266.05],[266.05,266.46],[266.46,266.67],[266.67,266.94],[266.94,267.07],[267.07,267.96],[267.96,268.16],[268.16,268.64],[268.64,268.91],[268.91,269.25],[269.25,269.59],[269.59,270.06],[270.06,270.88],[271.12,271.32],[271.32,271.73],[271.73,272.48],[272.48,272.68],[272.68,272.95],[272.95,273.09],[273.09,273.7],[273.7,273.97],[273.97,274.11],[274.11,274.51],[274.51,274.85],[274.85,275.13]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Local offices matter as much as Congress: district attorneys, sheriffs and city councils decide how police calls are handled.","start":275.91,"end":283.39,"words":[[275.96,276.37],[276.37,276.78],[276.78,277.06],[277.06,277.19],[277.19,277.47],[277.47,277.6],[277.6,278.15],[278.39,278.94],[278.94,279.42],[279.66,280.07],[280.07,280.27],[280.27,280.55],[280.55,281.1],[281.1,281.51],[281.51,281.71],[281.71,282.05],[282.05,282.33],[282.33,282.4],[282.4,282.94]]}]}